  "client_view_text": " In this way, a write-ahead log can prevent the loss of data that would be lost in the event of a crash.\nWhen a database updates the data, it first writes the changes to the write-ahead log. The write-ahead log is a record of all the changes that have been made to the data, in the order they were made. This means that even if the database crashes before it has a chance to update the data in memory, the write-ahead log still contains a record of the changes that need to be made. After the crash, the database can recover by re-applying the changes recorded in the write-ahead log to the In this detail, you need to explain the log file's structure, the redo phase, and how consistency is restored.\n## Step 1: Understand the purpose of a write-ahead log\nA write-ahead log (WAL) is a mechanism used by databases to ensure data consistency and durability in the event of a system crash or failure. The primary purpose of a WAL is to record all changes made to the database before they are written to the main database files.\n\n## Step 2: Explain the structure of the log file\nThe log file is structured as a sequence of log records, each of which represents a single database operation, such as a write or update. Each log record contains a sequence number, the type of operation (e.g., insert, update, delete), and any relevant data required to perform the operation. The log records are written in a specific order to ensure that they can be applied in a consistent manner.\n\n## Step 3: Describe the redo phase\nIn the event of a system crash, the database will enter a redo phase, during which it will read the log records from the log file and apply them to the database. The redo phase is responsible for recovering the database to a consistent state. The database will start by applying the log records in"
